<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu Amr al-Dani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">981–1053</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu Amr al-Dani (981–1053) was an Andalusi scholar of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, who settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denia">Dénia</a> under its ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mujahid-al-amiri">Mujahid al-Amiri</a>. He wrote more than a hundred works on Quran recitation, orthography and the seven canonical readings, of which al-Taysir became a standard textbook. His work shaped the teaching of Quranic recitation across the western Islamic world for centuries.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Al-Dānī, also called Ibn al-Ṣayrafī, was a Mālikī lawyer and traditionist as well as a Qurʾānic reciter. He was born in the village of Qūta Rāsha, a suburb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, into a family related to the reigning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad dynasty</a>. The main source for his life is a short autobiography preserved in the biographical dictionary of Yāqūt. By his own account he began his formal education in the seminaries of Córdoba at the age of fourteen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On 29 September 1006 he set out for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a>, where he studied ḥadīth, then moved after four months to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, and the next year made the pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>. In Mecca Abū Muslim Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Kātib introduced him to the seven canonical readings of Abū Bakr Ibn Mujāhid's Kitāb al-sabʿa. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> in August 1009, just as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> uprising and civil war began that would end the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad dynasty</a>.</p><p>After four years of turmoil he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>, stayed there seven years and then lived at an unidentified place called al-Wuṭṭa. In 1018 he moved to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denia">Dénia</a> under Mujāhid al-ʿĀmirī, from which his name al-Dānī, the one from Dénia, comes, and spent eight years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Mallorca</a> before returning to the capital in 1026 to teach and write. His school of recitation there drew students from far away.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denia">Dénia</a> on 8 February 1053. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> ʿAlī ibn Mujāhid Iqbāl al-Dawla led his funeral procession through the crowds, and he was buried at the Bāb Indāra; for his piety he was held to be one whose prayers are answered. Of his works 73 are known and about a third published, and al-Dhahabī counted 120. In the 12th century al-Shāṭibī put his manual of the seven readings into verse as the Shāṭibiyya and did the same for his treatise on Qurʾānic orthography, Kitāb al-Muqniʿ, to which al-Dānī had added the Kitāb al-Naqṭ on diacritics.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Al-Dani” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The port traded across the western Mediterranean, and the court supported Quranic scholarship: <b>Abu Amr al-Dani</b>, the leading authority on Quran recitation, worked there.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-denia">Taifa of Dénia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denia">Dénia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.84,0.11&amp;year=1027" aria-label="Dénia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4553 770 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Abu Amr al-Dani"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5003 1040h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5003 1040h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=38.84,0.11&amp;year=1027">What was here in 1027? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
